pancake is not released yet. Pin a commit when you add it to a project; the
public API can still change.

## In Zig

pancake is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) package. Add a pinned checkout to
your build:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake#COMMIT_SHA
```

Replace `COMMIT_SHA` with the revision you want to pin.

```zig
const pancake = b.dependency("pancake", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
exe.root_module.addImport("pancake", pancake.module("pancake"));
```

The client itself is one object and a handful of verbs. Here is the entire
round-trip:

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
>
> // Plain TCP uses a cleartext `host:port` locator.
> var tcp_resident = pancake.net.tcp.Resident{ .io = io };
> const partner = pancake.net.tcp.at(designator);
>
> const session = try pancake.driver.connect(
>     allocator,
>     &.{tcp_resident.netlayer()},
>     partner,
>     .ephemeral,
>     // Never leave a network wait unbounded.
>     .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
> );
> defer session.deinit();
>
> const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
> defer arena.deinit();
> const settled = try session.call(greeter, .{
>     .verb = "greet",
>     .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
> }, arena.allocator());
> switch (settled) {
>     .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
>     .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
>     // `driver.Session` redeems handoffs before returning.
>     .give => unreachable,
> }
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
> // In-repo builds only: `iroh` is wiring beside the package, not part of it.
> // From C and the bindings this transport is `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`.
> const iroh = @import("iroh");
>
> // Discovery resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> var iroh_resident = try iroh.Resident.init(io, null, .{ .reachability = .discovery });
> defer iroh_resident.deinit();
> const partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> const session = try pancake.driver.connectUriOwned(
>     allocator,
>     &.{iroh_resident.netlayer()},
>     partner,
>     .ephemeral,
>     .{},
> );
> defer session.deinit();
>
> const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
> defer arena.deinit();
> const settled = try session.call(greeter, .{
>     .verb = "greet",
>     .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
> }, arena.allocator());
> switch (settled) {
>     .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
>     .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
>     // `driver.Session` redeems handoffs before returning.
>     .give => unreachable,
> }
> ```

`io` is a `std.Io` instance. Create it from `std.Io.Threaded`:

```zig
var threaded = std.Io.Threaded.init(allocator, .{});
const io = threaded.io();
```

`designator` is the address string, such as `"127.0.0.1:9099"`. The two
timeout values keep network waits finite.

`.ephemeral` is the session identity, a throwaway keypair minted for this
connection. The arena owns reply allocations; dropping it frees them. This
owning `Session` also redeems three-party handoffs before returning, so a call
settles with the introduced capability rather than handoff bytes.

See the complete client and server in
[`01_connect_and_serve.zig`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig).
The iroh tab uses the same session calls with a node-id locator; the
[iroh guide]($link.page('transports/iroh')) covers discovery and deployment.

For a live partner to dial while you write this, build the
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')), then run:

```sh
zig-out/bin/pancake ocapn listen --publish greeter
```

It prints a locator serving the same greeter this client fetches.

## From C, or any FFI host

The same library is a plain C ABI centered on `PancakeHost`. Your runtime owns
the event loop and transport connection; it feeds bytes into a Host session,
drains Host actions, and writes the resulting frames. Capabilities are one
opaque Host-scoped `PancakeReference`, regardless of which session projects
them. The surface uses opaque handles, fixed-width return codes, `ptr + len`
views, and explicit release. That is the shape every binding wraps. Build,
ownership, and a first program:
[consuming pancake from C](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md).

## In Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket

Each binding wraps the same C ABI with an idiomatic API. Start with the
[bindings overview]($link.page('bindings')), then choose:
[Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')),
[TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts')),
[Python]($link.page('bindings/python')), or
[Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')).

For the browser rather than a server, the TypeScript binding also compiles to
WebAssembly as `@pancake/web`; the page always dials rather than listens.
Start at [In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')).

## Then go deeper

If capabilities, locators, pipelining, or three-party handoff are new to you,
read [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) first. Those are the hard ideas, once.
When the layer names blur (`Session`, `Host`, `Peer`),
[modules]($link.page('modules')) sorts them out, and
[Surfaces]($link.page('surfaces')) lists every way to hold the library.
